{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>To the princes of Balak</b> – According to Rashi's opinion (Rashi on Numbers 22:7), the elders of Midian went on their way, as he said to them, \"Lodge here tonight.\" This is possible, for when he mentioned to them, \"as YHWH speaks to me\" (Numbers 22:8), they did not wish to wait for him, for they said: \"This Name has been Israel's help from eternity; He is the one who brought them out of Egypt and performed signs and wonders for them.\" For the elders of Midian were wise and knew all the affairs of Moses with Jethro, the great one of their land, but Scripture did not mention this. And Ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 22:13) that Scripture did not mention them because Balak is the principal and he is the master of the mission, and likewise \"and the princes of Moab sat\" (ibid.), for the essence of the mission was from their lords. But the correct explanation is that the elders of Midian, and perhaps they are the former kings, came from their land to Balak to take counsel concerning the matter of Israel, and they all took counsel together to send to Balaam. Now Balak sent his princes, his judges and his wise men, and they went with the elders of Midian to the land of Midian, for from there was their route to Balaam's city, and the elders of Midian remained in their city, and the princes of Balak, who are the emissaries alone, went on to Aram. This is the sense of \"and the elders of Moab went and the elders of Midian\" — for they all went from before Balak as far as Midian; \"with charms in their hands, and they came to Balaam\" — this refers back to the elders of Moab, concerning whom it says \"and he sent messengers\" (Numbers 22:5), not to the elders of Midian." "<b>To go with you</b> – But with great princes. We learn that his spirit was haughty, and he did not wish to say that he was at the disposal of the Place, but in a language of haughtiness. Therefore, \"Balak again sent princes\" — this is Rashi's language (Rashi on Numbers 22:13). But this is not correct, for all his glory was to boast and to magnify himself by the matter of the Name. Furthermore, he would not have thought that He would grant him permission to go with other honored princes. Rather, his meaning is that the Name does not desire him to go at all. Now Balak suspected him of saying this in order to increase his fee, and therefore he said to him: \"Why did you not come to me? Am I truly unable to honor you?\" (Numbers 22:37). Therefore he added, sending many honored princes to show him that he greatly desired him, and vowed to give him wealth and riches according to all that he would command and decree upon him. And Balaam answered them: \"If Balak gives me etc., I cannot transgress the word of YHWH\" (Numbers 22:18) — for He is my Elohim and I cannot do anything small or great if I transgress His word, for it is in His name that I act. Or he may say: I cannot transgress His word in any matter small or great, for He is my Elohim and I am His servant."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The phrase 'שב לזקני מואב' (literally 'it returned to the elders of Moab') is understood contextually as referring the subject of the verb in v. 7 back to the elders of Moab specifically."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:56.269585114Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}